{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjsYpbA2bFR9xPCPV9Tm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12954,"cache_read_input_tokens":307650,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12954},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c4983f3c-8ab3-4cb5-bbfd-23a240d1dce6","uuid":"d8293b17-3e1c-4cd5-b42a-306fa41846f6","timestamp":"2026-11-05T13:37:47.413Z","request_id":"req_011CgjsYpJY7kijDEFcMUnXv","thinking_duration_ms":3409,"thinking_display":"updates"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjsYpbA2bFR9xPCPV9Tm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Mm7sb1RWmUT6hRuV8gcVEh","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat > src/data/story/script1.js <<'EOF'\n// Chapters 1 and 2. A tiny script format: \"@stage x\", \"@card A|B\", \"@cine x\", \"who expr: line\", \"? choice => Quill's answer | choice => answer\".\n(() => {\nconst G = (window.GAME ||= {}), S = (G.SCRIPT ||= {});\nS.c1_before = `\n@card Chapter 1|First Incision\n@stage scrub\n@cine scrubin\nquill neutral: Night shift, Doctor. Theatre Three. You are scrubbing the wrong arm.\nvale shock: Sorry! Sorry. There are two arms. I knew that. I have read about arms.\nquill neutral: Second-year resident, first solo. I have passed instruments for thirty-one years.\nquill neutral: I have seen worse. He was a plumber.\nvale neutral: Is the attending coming? Dr. Oyelaran said she'd maybe, possibly, come down?\nquill neutral: The Chief says a surgeon who needs a chaperone should have been a florist.\n@stage theatre\nagnes smile: Is that the young lady? You look about twelve. Mind my glass, dear.\nvale neutral: Mrs. Pellow, you fell through a greenhouse roof. We need to take the glass out.\nagnes smile: Well, keep it. My grandchildren are making a mosaic. It's going to be a heron.\n? Of course. Every piece. => She said every piece, Doctor. I shall fetch a nicer dish. | Mrs. Pellow, it's evidence of a fall. => It is evidence of a heron, Doctor.\nquill neutral: Under now. Breathe, Doctor. One of you should.\nvale shock: Okay okay okay. Sorry! Sorry. She can't hear me. Sorry.\n`;\nS.c1_after = `\n@stage scrub\nvale sad: My hands won't stop shaking.\nquill neutral: They stop around year thirty, Doctor. I'm told.\nvale neutral: Was that... did I do that right? You'd tell me?\nquill neutral: Eleven pieces of glass in a dish, one patient breathing. I have labeled the dish.\npercy smile: Vale! A forearm. Well done. They'll be letting you near a knee soon.\nvale neutral: Hi, Percy. It was actually quite a lot of glass.\npercy smile: I'm sure it was. I did a bowel resection before dinner. Don't stop. It's sweet.\nquill neutral: Dr. Whitlock has the steadiest hands in the building. It is a remarkable thing.\nquill neutral: He used to shake too, Doctor. Rather more than you. Until about last March.\n`;\nS.c2_before = `\n@stage radiology\nodalys smile: Okay so don't freak out, but Dr. Whitlock's chart is wrong and I brought you a donut.\nvale shock: Wrong how? Which one? Is the donut a bribe?\nodalys smile: Desmond Achebe. Trombone. Percy wrote hernia, in pen, like a man with no fear.\npercy smile: Hernia. Obviously. I saw him for ninety seconds. It was more than enough.\nodalys neutral: Look at the film. Bottom right. Tell me a hernia casts a round shadow like that.\nvale neutral: A surgeon who argues with a chart... is that allowed?\noyelaran neutral: A surgeon who argues with a chart. How tiresome. Do it again.\nvale shock: Chief! I didn't, I wasn't, hi. Sorry.\noyelaran neutral: Your chart, Dr. Vale. Your lightbox. Your mistake, should it be one. I shall watch.\n`;\nS.c2_after = `\n@stage gallery\nslide smile: A cork. From my spit valve. I swallowed that in 1987, at the Hall, mid-solo.\nslide smile: I wondered where that went. I finished the solo, too. Don't tell the band.\nvale smile: Mr. Achebe, it grew a whole mass around it. You had a tumor around a cork.\nquill neutral: I have labeled it, Doctor. \"Cork. Brass section. Do not return to patient.\"\nslide smile: Return it. I'm having it mounted.\npercy angry: Hernias can present atypically. I'd like that noted.\noyelaran neutral: It is noted, Dr. Whitlock. In pen.\noyelaran neutral: Dr. Vale. You had a nice clean ring cut. You also apologized to the mesentery.\nvale sad: It was there and I was... it seemed rude not to.\noyelaran smile: Keep the hands. Lose the apology. Good night.\n`;\nS.c3_before = `\n@stage harbor\n@cine gurney\nodalys shock: Okay so there's a thing on the radio! Crane Nine came down on the Anselm docks!\nvale shock: How many? Who's on call? Who's senior? Quill, who's senior?\nquill neutral: Dr. Oyelaran is in Geneva. Dr. Whitlock has \"gone for coffee.\" You are senior, Doctor.\n@stage theatre\ntam smile: Hallo, wee doctor. I'm Big Tam. Foreman. That was my crane. Is the crew all right?\nvale neutral: Sir, please don't sit up. You have a piece of the crane in you.\ntam smile: Aye. It's a good crane. Tell my Morag I'll be late for the tea.\nquill neutral: Three arterial tears. Pulse fifty-five. I mention it only in passing.\n? Nobody dies tonight. => That is a large sentence, Doctor. Scalpel. | I can't do this alone. => You aren't. I have my knitting in the corridor, in case it's long.\n`;\nS.c3_after = `\n@stage scrub\nvale sad: He died. Quill, he died on the table, and then he... didn't. And then there was rain.\nvale neutral: The rain in the window stopped. It just hung there. I sealed six ruptures.\nquill neutral: How long did that feel, Doctor?\nvale neutral: A minute? Maybe a minute?\nquill neutral: Eleven seconds. We don't discuss it in the lift.\nvale shock: We don't... what do you mean? Quill, what do you mean, \"we\"?\n@stage office\noyelaran neutral: You sealed six ruptures in eleven seconds, Dr. Vale. I read the monitor log myself.\nvale sad: Chief, I don't know what happened. I think I need to be checked for something.\noyelaran neutral: There is an old word in theatre. It is not said in the lift, and it is not on any form.\noyelaran smile: The Steady. Mr. Quill has passed instruments to three who had it. Keep your hands warm.\n`;\nS.c4_before = `\n@card Chapter 2|The Quiet Ones\n@stage ward\nned smile: Morning! Fine day for it. Could someone pull this out? I've a tide at four.\nvale shock: Sir! Captain! There is a gaff through your side! A gaff is a very large hook!\nned smile: Is there? So there is. Doesn't hurt a bit. I've never felt better. Whistled all the way in.\n@stage radiology\nodalys shock: Okay so don't freak out, but your X-ray is humming.\nvale neutral: X-rays don't hum, Odalys. Do they? Is that a thing?\nodalys neutral: Triage says superficial. Look beside the spine. Point at what you see. It moved.\nquill neutral: Bring a scalpel for the barbs, Doctor. And something for your nerves. Mine are spoken for.\n`;\nS.c4_after = `\n@stage theatre\nvale shock: Did you hear that? In there, under the tissue. Quill. It was singing. Three notes.\nquill sad: ...\nvale shock: You've got something dry to say. You always do. Quill? Say something dry.\nquill sad: ...Dressing, Doctor.\n@stage radiology\nodalys neutral: Okay. So I ran the tissue. It's not in any database. It isn't in the bad databases.\nodalys smile: It feeds on pain signals, Immy. That's why he whistled. It ate his whole gaff.\n@stage office\nvale neutral: Chief, it's alive and it sings and it eats pain. We have to tell somebody.\noyelaran angry: Not a word, Dr. Vale. Not until I have spoken to the Foundation. That is an instruction.\nvale sad: The Foundation? Why would the people who paid for the new wing need to...\noyelaran neutral: Close the door on your way out. Good night.\n`;\nS.c5_before = `\n@stage ward\nilse smile: You're the doctor? Please don't. I know what Mother signed. I don't want this.\nvale neutral: Ms. Brandt, you can't feel your left hand. You play the violin.\nilse smile: I can't feel it. Isn't it wonderful? Eleven years of tendon pain, gone. I play for hours.\nvale sad: Your fingers are bleeding on the strings. You can't feel that either.\nilse smile: I've never been so happy. You've no idea. Everything is so very quiet now.\npercy neutral: Vale, you've been up for forty hours. It's a neuropathy. Let the girl alone.\nvale neutral: There's something long moving under her forearm, Percy. I can hear it.\npercy smile: You can hear it. Yes. Go to bed.\nquill neutral: Fine mode today, Doctor. There is a nerve in there that plays Brahms.\n`;\nS.c5_after = `\n@stage ward\nilse sad: It hurts. Oh, it hurts. It's all of it, all at once, all eleven years.\nvale sad: I'm so sorry. I'm so sorry, I can give you something for...\nilse sad: No. No, don't you dare. I can feel my fingers. Thank you. Oh, thank you. It hurts.\n@stage radiology\nodalys shock: Okay so I made a wall. String, photographs, the works. Look. Every single case.\nodalys neutral: Harrow, Brandt, nine more in the ER log. All treated at an Anodyne clinic. All on Lull.\nvale neutral: The miracle analgesic. The billboards. \"A life without pain.\"\nodalys neutral: It really does work, is the thing. That's the scary part. It really, really works.\npercy angry: You've been up for forty hours and you've got a corkboard. Listen to yourselves.\nquill neutral: Dr. Whitlock's hands are remarkably steady, for this hour. I mention it only in passing.\n`;\nS.c6_before = `\n@stage gala\nhale smile: Friends. Pain is the last disease. Tonight, in this wing, we begin to cure it.\nhale smile: I owe this hospital everything. And I owe its Chief of Surgery rather more than that.\noyelaran neutral: You owe me a roof, Lucian. Raise your glass and be brief.\nvale neutral: Chief, please. I've got eleven patients and a corkboard. Just look at the film.\noyelaran sad: Not here, Dr. Vale. Not... here. Is it warm? I find it is rather...\n@cine galacollapse\nvale shock: CHIEF!\nhale sad: Someone call a doctor. Oh, Marguerite. I do hope you aren't in any pain.\n@stage theatre\nquill sad: It's the Chief, Doctor. So. No pressure. There's a note on her chart.\nvale sad: \"Do not be sentimental.\" In pen. Of course it's in pen.\nvale neutral: Scalpel, Mr. Quill. Please. And thank you. And sorry. Scalpel.\n`;\nS.c6_after = `\n@stage ward\noyelaran sad: Dr. Vale. You stitch like a nervous seamstress. It will hold.\nvale sad: You knew. Chief, you were taking it. Your hands. You were on Lull.\noyelaran sad: Arthritis, for nine years. And no roof. Lucian offered me both. I took them.\noyelaran neutral: The thing is called HUSH. Hyperplastic Undifferentiated Synaptic Hive. He built it.\noyelaran neutral: They sing, Doctor. So we name them like songs. I have known since spring.\nvale angry: You told me not to say a word. People are dying, and they're smiling.\noyelaran sad: Yes. That is why you must go to someone who is not me. Find Sokolova.\noyelaran neutral: Dr. Yevgenia Sokolova. Hospital ship Samaritan's Wake. She has the Steady too. Not like yours.\n@cine traydrop\nquill shock: ...\nvale shock: Quill? You dropped a tray. You've never dropped a tray. It's been thirty-one years.\nquill neutral: So it has, Doctor. Mind the forceps.\nhale smile: On the ward TV, a warm voice: \"I hear Dr. Oyelaran is recovering. Wonderful.\"\n`;\n})();\nEOF\ncat > src/data/story/script2.js <<'EOF'\n// Chapters 3 and 4, and the ending.\n(() => {\nconst G = (window.GAME ||= {}), S = (G.SCRIPT ||= {});\nS.c7_before = `\n@card Chapter 3|Second Look\n@stage harbor\n@cine stormship\nvale shock: Is that normal? Quill! Is the launch supposed to go vertically?\nquill neutral: I was merchant navy nine years, Doctor. Hold the rail. Do not look at the rail.\n@stage shipward\nsokolova smile: You're late, Bartholomew.\nquill neutral: Thirty-one years, Yevgenia.\nsokolova smile: And you've brought me a child in clogs. Good. Children have knees. Hold this lamp, child.\nvale neutral: Dr. Sokolova, Dr. Oyelaran sent me, there's a parasite, it sings, I have a corkboard.\nsokolova neutral: Later. The winch has come off its mount and it has come off onto Yusuf.\nyusuf sad: Doctora. I left the brake off. I think. Is it bad? I can't tell. Is it bad?\nsokolova smile: At my age, dear, everything happens twice. Scalpel. Watch closely, child. Watch my hands.\n`;\nS.c7_after = `\n@stage shipward\nvale shock: You slipped. The ship rolled, your hand slipped, I saw the cut. Then it wasn't there.\nsokolova smile: No. Again. That's all it is, child. You stretch a moment. I take it back.\nvale neutral: That's impossible. How often can you... does it cost you anything?\nsokolova neutral: I am sixty-seven. I remember every operation twice, and some of them I lost once.\nquill neutral: She did this with a spoon, in the navy.\nsokolova smile: It was a very good spoon. Now, Bartholomew. Your Chief sent the girl, so it's time.\nyusuf smile: Doctora! I can feel my leg. It's terrible. May I have a sandwich?\nsokolova smile: Pain, child. You hear that? That's the sound of a boy who will be fine.\n`;\nS.c8_before = `\n@stage chapel\nperpetua smile: God's peace to you, Doctor. Mind the step. I've brought six more aboard this evening.\nvale sad: Sister, your own liver's failing. Your chart looks like a shipwreck. Aren't you frightened?\nperpetua smile: I've run the seamen's hospice for forty years. Do you know how they used to die, child?\nperpetua smile: Screaming. And now they go smiling, holding my hand, one after another. It is a mercy.\nsokolova neutral: It's a parasite, Perpetua. It repeats. Cut it once, it plays the whole thing over.\nperpetua neutral: Then you'll cut it. I've signed. But I'd like to talk to you, after, about what you've done.\nquill neutral: Three lobes. It will start again if you're slow. I understand it's musical.\n`;\nS.c8_after = `\n@stage chapel\nperpetua sad: It's back. The ache in my hip. The sciatica. Every one of those men, I remember them now.\nperpetua angry: You'd give them back their pain? All of my dying men? That is what you came for?\nvale sad: I... Sister, I don't know. They were happy. They really were. I'm not sure I can...\nsokolova neutral: I'd give them back their choice.\nperpetua neutral: Hah. A choice between what and what, Yevgenia? You have never held their hands.\nsokolova neutral: No. I have only ever held their insides. It's a good argument. Lose it to me again sometime.\n? She has a point. => She has had it for forty years, Doctor. It has not gotten smaller. | Pain means you're still here. => The staff have a saying, Doctor. Don't let it go quiet.\n`;\nS.c9_before = `\n@stage shipward\ncrane sad: Dr. Crane. Hollis. I ran Lull's second trial. Please don't touch my chest.\nsokolova neutral: You've come aboard in a Force Eight with one small bag to confess, and you're sweating.\ncrane sad: Dr. Hale is a thoughtful man. He gave me a new pacemaker the day I resigned.\ncrane shock: If my heart stops, it detonates. If the can is forced, it detonates. It's quite a large can.\nvale shock: On the ship? Should we be... should he be... get him off the ship!\nsokolova smile: We are doctors.\nquill neutral: I'll fetch the other forceps.\ncrane sad: The wires. Cut triangle, circle, square. I'm almost sure. I designed the board. I think.\nsokolova neutral: Almost, he says. A second opinion, child. Bring the Sonar.\n`;\nS.c9_after = `\n@stage shipward\ncrane sad: The can's in the sea? It's in the sea. I can feel my chest. It hurts terribly. Thank you.\nvale neutral: Dr. Crane, what started this? Who was the first? Where does all of it come from?\ncrane sad: He had a daughter. Clementine. Nine years old. Bone disease. She screamed for two years.\ncrane sad: He's the kindest man I ever met. He made HUSH so that she could stop. And she stopped.\ncrane shock: It's in her still. All of it comes from her. Every colony, every strain. It's her song.\ncrane sad: Everything is singing to her. And she's nineteen now. And it's nearly done growing.\nsokolova neutral: Then we sail for Port Anselm. Bartholomew, wake the engineer. Child, sleep.\nvale neutral: I can't sleep. Can I ask you something, Quill? Thirty-one years. Why did you go?\nquill neutral: I didn't, Doctor. She did. Theatre Three still has her apron on the hook.\n`;\nS.c10_before = `\n@card Chapter 4|Coda\n@stage ward\npercy neutral: Vale. You're back. Everyone's been... it's fine. Everything's fine. I wrote it all up.\nvale neutral: Percy, you look awful. When did you last sleep? Your eyes are doing something.\npercy smile: Do you know, I've never told you this. You've got better hands than I have. It's fine.\npercy smile: It doesn't bother me. Nothing bothers me. Isn't that... oh. It's finally quiet.\n@cine percycollapse\nvale shock: Percy! PERCY! Somebody! He's still smiling, why is he still smiling!\n@stage radiology\nodalys sad: Okay. So. He charted himself. In his own hand. It says migraine. He's been on Lull since March.\nodalys neutral: Look at the film. Point at it. I'm not going to say what I think it is. It's looking at me.\nsokolova neutral: Two surgeons, child. One table. Try not to be sentimental. I shall take the first cut.\n`;\nS.c10_after = `\n@stage ward\nvale sad: Percy? Percy, it's Immy. Can you hear me? Do you know where you are?\npercy angry: Your stitches... are crooked.\nvale smile: They are. They're so crooked. I did them crooked. I'm so sorry. I'm crying, this is crying.\npercy sad: It hurts, Vale. Everything hurts. I've been so very frightened. For such a long time.\npercy neutral: I should've said. It was never a hernia. Do you want that in writing?\nquill neutral: In pen, Doctor.\nsokolova neutral: Good. Now the bad news, children. Look at the ER board.\nodalys shock: Okay so everybody freak out. Every Anodyne patient in the city's failing. All of them. At once.\n`;\nS.c11_before = `\n@stage ward\nhale sad: Please. Someone. Please. She's stopped answering. Someone please take her.\nvale shock: Dr. Hale. You came here? You carried her? In the rain? All the way from the east wing?\nhale sad: I only wanted her not to hurt. Doctor, you have to understand. I only wanted her not to hurt.\nclementine smile: Hello. It's all right, Father. Everyone's singing. Can you hear it? It's so beautiful now.\nsokolova neutral: It's wrapped around her heart, Lucian. It's conducting the whole city from in there.\nhale sad: Pain is the last disease, Doctor. I only ask you to stop defending it. Save her. Please.\n@cine finaltable\noyelaran neutral: I'm in the gallery. I've got a wheelchair and an opinion. Do not make me use either.\npercy neutral: I've got the monitors. Your pressure readings'll be right. For once. In pen.\nodalys smile: I've got the film and six donuts. Okay so don't freak out.\nquill neutral: Scalpel, Doctors. Whichever of you. I've passed instruments to four of you now.\nvale neutral: Don't let it go quiet, Quill.\nquill smile: No, Doctor. Not tonight.\n`;\nS.c11_after = `\n@stage theatre\nclementine shock: AAH! AAAH! Oh, it, oh, what, oh, Father, it, I...\nclementine smile: I can feel my hands. I can feel my hands! They hurt! Is this what it... they're so warm!\nhale sad: Clementine. Oh, my girl. Oh, there you are. I'm so sorry. There you are.\n@cine erchaos\n@stage ward\nagnes angry: Doctor! I have a headache! My whole arm aches! I demand to be seen!\nvale smile: Wonderful!\nslide angry: My spit valve hurts! I don't know how! I want my money back!\nvale smile: That's wonderful! Next!\nned angry: There was a GAFF in me! Who authorized that! I felt NOTHING and I demand to feel it now!\noyelaran smile: Listen to them. The loudest, angriest, happiest ward in the North Atlantic.\nhale neutral: I'll be in the east wing, Doctors, when the police arrive. I'll leave the doors unlocked.\nhale neutral: Doctor? You were right to defend it. I still wish that you hadn't had to.\nsokolova smile: Well, Bartholomew. Apron's still on the hook, I hear.\nquill neutral: Thirty-one years, Yevgenia. I've had it washed.\nvale neutral: Quill. Look. My hands. I think they've...\nquill neutral: They've stopped shaking, Doctor.\nvale neutral: ...No, they haven't.\nquill smile: Good.\n`;\n})();\nEOF\ncat > src/data/story/flow.js <<'EOF'\n// Compiles the script format into E.Story nodes, and lays out the story's order. Adding a case: a script, a line here.\n(() => {\nconst G = (window.GAME ||= {});\nG.FLOW = []; for (let i = 1; i <= 11; i++) { G.FLOW.push({ k: 'story', id: 'c' + i + '_before' }); G.FLOW.push({ k: 'case', id: 'c' + i }); G.FLOW.push({ k: 'story', id: 'c' + i + '_after' }); if (i < 11) G.FLOW.push({ k: 'ward', map: i >= 6 && i <= 8 ? 'ship' : 'ward3', after: i }); } G.FLOW.push({ k: 'credits' });\nG.compileStory = () => { const nodes = {}; for (const id in G.SCRIPT) { const out = []; let extra = 0; const lines = G.SCRIPT[id].split('\\n').map(l => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);\n    lines.forEach((l) => { if (l[0] === '@') { const sp = l.indexOf(' '), k = l.slice(1, sp), v = l.slice(sp + 1); if (k === 'card') out.push({ event: 'card', arg: v, wait: true }); else if (k === 'stage') out.push({ event: 'stage', arg: v }); else if (k === 'cine') out.push({ event: 'cine', arg: v, wait: true }); return; }\n      if (l[0] === '?') { const opts = l.slice(1).split('|').map(s => s.trim().split('=>').map(q => q.trim())), back = id + '#' + (extra++); const prev = out[out.length - 1]; const who = 'vale'; const choices = opts.map(([text, reply], i) => { const n = back + 'r' + i; nodes[n] = [{ event: 'look', who: 'quill', expr: 'neutral' }, { who: 'quill', say: reply }, { goto: back }]; return { text, goto: n }; }); out.push({ event: 'look', who: 'vale', expr: 'neutral' }); out.push({ who: 'vale', say: '...', choices }); nodes[back] = []; out.push({ __cont: back }); return; }\n      const m = l.match(/^(\\w+) (\\w+): (.*)$/); if (!m) throw new Error('bad script line: ' + l); out.push({ event: 'look', who: m[1], expr: m[2] }); out.push({ who: m[1], say: m[3] }); });\n    // a choice's replies come back to the rest of the scene\n    let cur = out, res = []; const finish = (arr, name) => { nodes[name] = arr; };\n    const flat = []; let target = id; nodes[target] = flat; for (const st of out) { if (st.__cont) { const nx = nodes[st.__cont]; target = st.__cont; nodes[target] = nx; cur = nx; continue; } (target === id ? flat : nodes[target]).push(st); } }\n  return nodes; };\nG.WARD_LINES = {\n  quill: ['Knit one. Purl one. Rest, Doctor. The next one is always worse.', 'I am making a scarf. It is for no one. It has been for no one since 1994.', 'Morale, Doctor. I have heard of it.'],\n  odalys: ['Okay so I have a theory, and it has a corkboard. Donut?', 'The vending machine hums in B-flat. I checked it. It is just a vending machine. I think.', 'I found your scan. You have nice bones, Immy. I mean that professionally.'],\n  percy: ['The machine took my coin. It would not dare take a second one.', 'Shouldn\\'t you be labeling something, Vale? I have a resection to think about.', 'I have not had a tremor in months. It is all in the wrists.', 'It is odd. It has been so quiet in here lately.'],\n  chief: ['The door says Do Not Knock. It has said so for eleven years.', 'A dry cough, from behind the door: \"Go and do something useful, Dr. Vale.\"', 'The cane is in the umbrella stand. That has never happened before.'],\n  agnes: ['The grandchildren brought begonias. They are enormous. I told them to put them by the glass.', 'The heron is coming along. It has one leg so far. Herons do, mostly.', 'Are you eating, dear? You look like you are eating a very small amount.', 'That arm aches when the rain comes in. I rather like knowing the weather.'],\n  slide: ['I practice with the mute in. Your nurse tells me it is an improvement.', 'I have had the cork mounted. It is on the bell of the horn. Very classy.', 'Long notes hold now. Forty seconds. Don\\'t tell my cardiologist.'],\n  tam: ['Tell you what, wee doctor. That crane is getting a stern talking to.', 'Morag brought the tea. It was late, but so was I.'],\n  ned: ['I can feel the tide in this side now. It is a nuisance. Very reliable.', 'No more whistling. It hurts when I whistle. I whistle anyway.'],\n  ilse: ['It hurts to hold the bow. I played for an hour. It was the worst hour. Listen to this.', 'Mother cried. I think I cried. I can tell now.'],\n  yusuf: ['I have asked for a third sandwich. The doctora says it is a medical necessity.', 'The ship rolls and I feel it in the leg. Now I always know the weather.'],\n  perpetua: ['I have not forgiven her. I would like you to tell her so.', 'Sit a moment, child. You look as though you have been holding something. Hand it here.'],\n  crane: ['I keep putting my hand on my chest. There is only me in there now.', 'I would like to sign something. A confession, a petition. Anything.'],\n};\nG.WARD_PEOPLE = { 1: ['agnes'], 2: ['agnes', 'slide'], 3: ['agnes', 'slide', 'tam'], 4: ['slide', 'tam', 'ned'], 5: ['tam', 'ned', 'ilse'], 6: ['ned', 'ilse'], 7: ['yusuf'], 8: ['yusuf', 'perpetua'], 9: ['agnes', 'ilse', 'crane'], 10: ['agnes', 'slide', 'tam', 'ned', 'ilse', 'yusuf', 'crane'] };\n})();\nEOF\ncat > src/data/barks.js <<'EOF'\n// Quill's lines by situation. Barks never pause play, and a shared pool keeps them from repeating fast.\n(() => {\nconst G = (window.GAME ||= {});\nG.BARKS = {\n  slip: ['That was the liver, Doctor. We keep that one.', 'A bold line. Not the one we drew.', 'I have seen worse. He was a plumber.', 'Noted, Doctor. I shall not write it down.', 'The other way would have been traditional.', 'Mm.', 'Breathe, Doctor. One of you should.', 'She will not feel that. You might.'],\n  tear: ['Slower. It is not going anywhere.', 'It tore. They do that when hurried.', 'That came out in two parts, Doctor.', 'Along the axis. The way it went in.'],\n  low: ['Pulse forty. I mention it only in passing.', 'Getting quiet, Doctor. Syringe.', 'I would dose the IV now, if I were you.', 'Thirty and falling. No rush. Some rush.', 'Stimulant, Doctor. He is using his up.'],\n  textbook: ['...Hm.', 'Acceptable.', 'I have seen that done worse. Rarely.', 'I shall tell no one. They would only ask you to do it again.', 'Nice.', 'That one goes in the book, Doctor.'],\n  idle: ['Any time, Doctor.', 'The next tool, if it pleases you.', 'I could recite something, while we wait.', 'He is not getting any younger.'],\n  phase: ['Next.', 'Good. Keep going.', 'Moving on.', 'That is one thing fixed. There are others.'],\n  flow: ['You are in it now, Doctor. Do not look down.', 'Steady hands tonight.', 'Keep that going.'],\n  heat: ['It runs hot, Doctor. Let it rest.', 'That is the cautery telling you something.'],\n  revived: ['Welcome back, sir. Doctor, you may breathe now.', 'He is with us. Do not make a habit of it.'],\n  gift: ['We don\\'t discuss it in the lift.', 'There it is.'],\n  look: ['No. Again.', 'At my age, everything happens twice.', 'I\\'ve seen this one.'],\n  bite: ['Something bit him. He has not noticed.', 'They are landing, Doctor. Watch the rings.'],\n  readout: ['{v}, Doctor.', 'Pulse {v}.', '{v}. Holding.', 'I have {v}, Doctor.'],\n  wrongwire: ['That was not the wire, Doctor. It is still a ship. For now.'],\n  loose: ['Loose. Take it out. Do it again.', 'That will not hold, Doctor.'],\n  close: ['Dressing.'],\n};\nG.LOST_LINES = { vitals: ['Time of death, oh-four-twelve. Shall we go again, Doctor?', 'He is gone, Doctor. Take a breath. Take it again.'], time: ['Time of death, oh-four-twelve.', 'We ran out of night, Doctor.'], bomb: ['The ship went, Doctor. I had just washed that apron. Again?'] };\nG.RANK_LINES = { S: ['I have nothing to add. Mark the date.', 'I passed instruments to three who could do that. Four.'], A: ['Very nearly dull, Doctor. That is high praise.', 'I would let you operate on my cat.'], B: ['It held. Most things do, if you stitch them.', 'A solid night. Go and shake somewhere private.'], C: ['The patient lived. We shall lead with that.', 'I have seen worse. He was a plumber.'], D: ['Alive is a grade, Doctor. It is the lowest one.', 'I shall file this under \"Eventually\".'] };\n})();\nEOF\n","description":"Write full story scripts, flow compiler and barks"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12954,"cache_read_input_tokens":307650,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12954},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c4983f3c-8ab3-4cb5-bbfd-23a240d1dce6","uuid":"716be9d1-c669-4e62-9a1d-70d9b86c4049","timestamp":"2026-11-05T13:39:52.682Z","request_id":"req_011CgjsYpJY7kijDEFcMUnXv","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Mm7sb1RWmUT6hRuV8gcVEh":{"command":"cat > src/data/story/script1.js <<'EOF'\n// Chapters 1 and 2. A tiny script format: \"@stage x\", \"@card A|B\", \"@cine x\", \"who expr: line\", \"? choice => Quill's answer | choice => answer\".\n(() => {\nconst G = (window.GAME ||= {}), S = (G.SCRIPT ||= {});\nS.c1_before = `\n@card Chapter 1|First Incision\n@stage scrub\n@cine scrubin\nquill neutral: Night shift, Doctor. Theatre Three. You are scrubbing the wrong arm.\nvale shock: Sorry! Sorry. There are two arms. I knew that. I have read about arms.\nquill neutral: Second-year resident, first solo. I have passed instruments for thirty-one years.\nquill neutral: I have seen worse. He was a plumber.\nvale neutral: Is the attending coming? Dr. Oyelaran said she'd maybe, possibly, come down?\nquill neutral: The Chief says a surgeon who needs a chaperone should have been a florist.\n@stage theatre\nagnes smile: Is that the young lady? You look about twelve. Mind my glass, dear.\nvale neutral: Mrs. Pellow, you fell through a greenhouse roof. We need to take the glass out.\nagnes smile: Well, keep it. My grandchildren are making a mosaic. It's going to be a heron.\n? Of course. Every piece. => She said every piece, Doctor. I shall fetch a nicer dish. | Mrs. Pellow, it's evidence of a fall. => It is evidence of a heron, Doctor.\nquill neutral: Under now. Breathe, Doctor. One of you should.\nvale shock: Okay okay okay. Sorry! Sorry. She can't hear me. Sorry.\n`;\nS.c1_after = `\n@stage scrub\nvale sad: My hands won't stop shaking.\nquill neutral: They stop around year thirty, Doctor. I'm told.\nvale neutral: Was that... did I do that right? You'd tell me?\nquill neutral: Eleven pieces of glass in a dish, one patient breathing. I have labeled the dish.\npercy smile: Vale! A forearm. Well done. They'll be letting you near a knee soon.\nvale neutral: Hi, Percy. It was actually quite a lot of glass.\npercy smile: I'm sure it was. I did a bowel resection before dinner. Don't stop. It's sweet.\nquill neutral: Dr. Whitlock has the steadiest hands in the building. It is a remarkable thing.\nquill neutral: He used to shake too, Doctor. Rather more than you. Until about last March.\n`;\nS.c2_before = `\n@stage radiology\nodalys smile: Okay so don't freak out, but Dr. Whitlock's chart is wrong and I brought you a donut.\nvale shock: Wrong how? Which one? Is the donut a bribe?\nodalys smile: Desmond Achebe. Trombone. Percy wrote hernia, in pen, like a man with no fear.\npercy smile: Hernia. Obviously. I saw him for ninety seconds. It was more than enough.\nodalys neutral: Look at the film. Bottom right. Tell me a hernia casts a round shadow like that.\nvale neutral: A surgeon who argues with a chart... is that allowed?\noyelaran neutral: A surgeon who argues with a chart. How tiresome. Do it again.\nvale shock: Chief! I didn't, I wasn't, hi. Sorry.\noyelaran neutral: Your chart, Dr. Vale. Your lightbox. Your mistake, should it be one. I shall watch.\n`;\nS.c2_after = `\n@stage gallery\nslide smile: A cork. From my spit valve. I swallowed that in 1987, at the Hall, mid-solo.\nslide smile: I wondered where that went. I finished the solo, too. Don't tell the band.\nvale smile: Mr. Achebe, it grew a whole mass around it. You had a tumor around a cork.\nquill neutral: I have labeled it, Doctor. \"Cork. Brass section. Do not return to patient.\"\nslide smile: Return it. I'm having it mounted.\npercy angry: Hernias can present atypically. I'd like that noted.\noyelaran neutral: It is noted, Dr. Whitlock. In pen.\noyelaran neutral: Dr. Vale. You had a nice clean ring cut. You also apologized to the mesentery.\nvale sad: It was there and I was... it seemed rude not to.\noyelaran smile: Keep the hands. Lose the apology. Good night.\n`;\nS.c3_before = `\n@stage harbor\n@cine gurney\nodalys shock: Okay so there's a thing on the radio! Crane Nine came down on the Anselm docks!\nvale shock: How many? Who's on call? Who's senior? Quill, who's senior?\nquill neutral: Dr. Oyelaran is in Geneva. Dr. Whitlock has \"gone for coffee.\" You are senior, Doctor.\n@stage theatre\ntam smile: Hallo, wee doctor. I'm Big Tam. Foreman. That was my crane. Is the crew all right?\nvale neutral: Sir, please don't sit up. You have a piece of the crane in you.\ntam smile: Aye. It's a good crane. Tell my Morag I'll be late for the tea.\nquill neutral: Three arterial tears. Pulse fifty-five. I mention it only in passing.\n? Nobody dies tonight. => That is a large sentence, Doctor. Scalpel. | I can't do this alone. => You aren't. I have my knitting in the corridor, in case it's long.\n`;\nS.c3_after = `\n@stage scrub\nvale sad: He died. Quill, he died on the table, and then he... didn't. And then there was rain.\nvale neutral: The rain in the window stopped. It just hung there. I sealed six ruptures.\nquill neutral: How long did that feel, Doctor?\nvale neutral: A minute? Maybe a minute?\nquill neutral: Eleven seconds. We don't discuss it in the lift.\nvale shock: We don't... what do you mean? Quill, what do you mean, \"we\"?\n@stage office\noyelaran neutral: You sealed six ruptures in eleven seconds, Dr. Vale. I read the monitor log myself.\nvale sad: Chief, I don't know what happened. I think I need to be checked for something.\noyelaran neutral: There is an old word in theatre. It is not said in the lift, and it is not on any form.\noyelaran smile: The Steady. Mr. Quill has passed instruments to three who had it. Keep your hands warm.\n`;\nS.c4_before = `\n@card Chapter 2|The Quiet Ones\n@stage ward\nned smile: Morning! Fine day for it. Could someone pull this out? I've a tide at four.\nvale shock: Sir! Captain! There is a gaff through your side! A gaff is a very large hook!\nned smile: Is there? So there is. Doesn't hurt a bit. I've never felt better. Whistled all the way in.\n@stage radiology\nodalys shock: Okay so don't freak out, but your X-ray is humming.\nvale neutral: X-rays don't hum, Odalys. Do they? Is that a thing?\nodalys neutral: Triage says superficial. Look beside the spine. Point at what you see. It moved.\nquill neutral: Bring a scalpel for the barbs, Doctor. And something for your nerves. Mine are spoken for.\n`;\nS.c4_after = `\n@stage theatre\nvale shock: Did you hear that? In there, under the tissue. Quill. It was singing. Three notes.\nquill sad: ...\nvale shock: You've got something dry to say. You always do. Quill? Say something dry.\nquill sad: ...Dressing, Doctor.\n@stage radiology\nodalys neutral: Okay. So I ran the tissue. It's not in any database. It isn't in the bad databases.\nodalys smile: It feeds on pain signals, Immy. That's why he whistled. It ate his whole gaff.\n@stage office\nvale neutral: Chief, it's alive and it sings and it eats pain. We have to tell somebody.\noyelaran angry: Not a word, Dr. Vale. Not until I have spoken to the Foundation. That is an instruction.\nvale sad: The Foundation? Why would the people who paid for the new wing need to...\noyelaran neutral: Close the door on your way out. Good night.\n`;\nS.c5_before = `\n@stage ward\nilse smile: You're the doctor? Please don't. I know what Mother signed. I don't want this.\nvale neutral: Ms. Brandt, you can't feel your left hand. You play the violin.\nilse smile: I can't feel it. Isn't it wonderful? Eleven years of tendon pain, gone. I play for hours.\nvale sad: Your fingers are bleeding on the strings. You can't feel that either.\nilse smile: I've never been so happy. You've no idea. Everything is so very quiet now.\npercy neutral: Vale, you've been up for forty hours. It's a neuropathy. Let the girl alone.\nvale neutral: There's something long moving under her forearm, Percy. I can hear it.\npercy smile: You can hear it. Yes. Go to bed.\nquill neutral: Fine mode today, Doctor. There is a nerve in there that plays Brahms.\n`;\nS.c5_after = `\n@stage ward\nilse sad: It hurts. Oh, it hurts. It's all of it, all at once, all eleven years.\nvale sad: I'm so sorry. I'm so sorry, I can give you something for...\nilse sad: No. No, don't you dare. I can feel my fingers. Thank you. Oh, thank you. It hurts.\n@stage radiology\nodalys shock: Okay so I made a wall. String, photographs, the works. Look. Every single case.\nodalys neutral: Harrow, Brandt, nine more in the ER log. All treated at an Anodyne clinic. All on Lull.\nvale neutral: The miracle analgesic. The billboards. \"A life without pain.\"\nodalys neutral: It really does work, is the thing. That's the scary part. It really, really works.\npercy angry: You've been up for forty hours and you've got a corkboard. Listen to yourselves.\nquill neutral: Dr. Whitlock's hands are remarkably steady, for this hour. I mention it only in passing.\n`;\nS.c6_before = `\n@stage gala\nhale smile: Friends. Pain is the last disease. Tonight, in this wing, we begin to cure it.\nhale smile: I owe this hospital everything. And I owe its Chief of Surgery rather more than that.\noyelaran neutral: You owe me a roof, Lucian. Raise your glass and be brief.\nvale neutral: Chief, please. I've got eleven patients and a corkboard. Just look at the film.\noyelaran sad: Not here, Dr. Vale. Not... here. Is it warm? I find it is rather...\n@cine galacollapse\nvale shock: CHIEF!\nhale sad: Someone call a doctor. Oh, Marguerite. I do hope you aren't in any pain.\n@stage theatre\nquill sad: It's the Chief, Doctor. So. No pressure. There's a note on her chart.\nvale sad: \"Do not be sentimental.\" In pen. Of course it's in pen.\nvale neutral: Scalpel, Mr. Quill. Please. And thank you. And sorry. Scalpel.\n`;\nS.c6_after = `\n@stage ward\noyelaran sad: Dr. Vale. You stitch like a nervous seamstress. It will hold.\nvale sad: You knew. Chief, you were taking it. Your hands. You were on Lull.\noyelaran sad: Arthritis, for nine years. And no roof. Lucian offered me both. I took them.\noyelaran neutral: The thing is called HUSH. Hyperplastic Undifferentiated Synaptic Hive. He built it.\noyelaran neutral: They sing, Doctor. So we name them like songs. I have known since spring.\nvale angry: You told me not to say a word. People are dying, and they're smiling.\noyelaran sad: Yes. That is why you must go to someone who is not me. Find Sokolova.\noyelaran neutral: Dr. Yevgenia Sokolova. Hospital ship Samaritan's Wake. She has the Steady too. Not like yours.\n@cine traydrop\nquill shock: ...\nvale shock: Quill? You dropped a tray. You've never dropped a tray. It's been thirty-one years.\nquill neutral: So it has, Doctor. Mind the forceps.\nhale smile: On the ward TV, a warm voice: \"I hear Dr. Oyelaran is recovering. Wonderful.\"\n`;\n})();\nEOF\ncat > src/data/story/script2.js <<'EOF'\n// Chapters 3 and 4, and the ending.\n(() => {\nconst G = (window.GAME ||= {}), S = (G.SCRIPT ||= {});\nS.c7_before = `\n@card Chapter 3|Second Look\n@stage harbor\n@cine stormship\nvale shock: Is that normal? Quill! Is the launch supposed to go vertically?\nquill neutral: I was merchant navy nine years, Doctor. Hold the rail. Do not look at the rail.\n@stage shipward\nsokolova smile: You're late, Bartholomew.\nquill neutral: Thirty-one years, Yevgenia.\nsokolova smile: And you've brought me a child in clogs. Good. Children have knees. Hold this lamp, child.\nvale neutral: Dr. Sokolova, Dr. Oyelaran sent me, there's a parasite, it sings, I have a corkboard.\nsokolova neutral: Later. The winch has come off its mount and it has come off onto Yusuf.\nyusuf sad: Doctora. I left the brake off. I think. Is it bad? I can't tell. Is it bad?\nsokolova smile: At my age, dear, everything happens twice. Scalpel. Watch closely, child. Watch my hands.\n`;\nS.c7_after = `\n@stage shipward\nvale shock: You slipped. The ship rolled, your hand slipped, I saw the cut. Then it wasn't there.\nsokolova smile: No. Again. That's all it is, child. You stretch a moment. I take it back.\nvale neutral: That's impossible. How often can you... does it cost you anything?\nsokolova neutral: I am sixty-seven. I remember every operation twice, and some of them I lost once.\nquill neutral: She did this with a spoon, in the navy.\nsokolova smile: It was a very good spoon. Now, Bartholomew. Your Chief sent the girl, so it's time.\nyusuf smile: Doctora! I can feel my leg. It's terrible. May I have a sandwich?\nsokolova smile: Pain, child. You hear that? That's the sound of a boy who will be fine.\n`;\nS.c8_before = `\n@stage chapel\nperpetua smile: God's peace to you, Doctor. Mind the step. I've brought six more aboard this evening.\nvale sad: Sister, your own liver's failing. Your chart looks like a shipwreck. Aren't you frightened?\nperpetua smile: I've run the seamen's hospice for forty years. Do you know how they used to die, child?\nperpetua smile: Screaming. And now they go smiling, holding my hand, one after another. It is a mercy.\nsokolova neutral: It's a parasite, Perpetua. It repeats. Cut it once, it plays the whole thing over.\nperpetua neutral: Then you'll cut it. I've signed. But I'd like to talk to you, after, about what you've done.\nquill neutral: Three lobes. It will start again if you're slow. I understand it's musical.\n`;\nS.c8_after = `\n@stage chapel\nperpetua sad: It's back. The ache in my hip. The sciatica. Every one of those men, I remember them now.\nperpetua angry: You'd give them back their pain? All of my dying men? That is what you came for?\nvale sad: I... Sister, I don't know. They were happy. They really were. I'm not sure I can...\nsokolova neutral: I'd give them back their choice.\nperpetua neutral: Hah. A choice between what and what, Yevgenia? You have never held their hands.\nsokolova neutral: No. I have only ever held their insides. It's a good argument. Lose it to me again sometime.\n? She has a point. => She has had it for forty years, Doctor. It has not gotten smaller. | Pain means you're still here. => The staff have a saying, Doctor. Don't let it go quiet.\n`;\nS.c9_before = `\n@stage shipward\ncrane sad: Dr. Crane. Hollis. I ran Lull's second trial. Please don't touch my chest.\nsokolova neutral: You've come aboard in a Force Eight with one small bag to confess, and you're sweating.\ncrane sad: Dr. Hale is a thoughtful man. He gave me a new pacemaker the day I resigned.\ncrane shock: If my heart stops, it detonates. If the can is forced, it detonates. It's quite a large can.\nvale shock: On the ship? Should we be... should he be... get him off the ship!\nsokolova smile: We are doctors.\nquill neutral: I'll fetch the other forceps.\ncrane sad: The wires. Cut triangle, circle, square. I'm almost sure. I designed the board. I think.\nsokolova neutral: Almost, he says. A second opinion, child. Bring the Sonar.\n`;\nS.c9_after = `\n@stage shipward\ncrane sad: The can's in the sea? It's in the sea. I can feel my chest. It hurts terribly. Thank you.\nvale neutral: Dr. Crane, what started this? Who was the first? Where does all of it come from?\ncrane sad: He had a daughter. Clementine. Nine years old. Bone disease. She screamed for two years.\ncrane sad: He's the kindest man I ever met. He made HUSH so that she could stop. And she stopped.\ncrane shock: It's in her still. All of it comes from her. Every colony, every strain. It's her song.\ncrane sad: Everything is singing to her. And she's nineteen now. And it's nearly done growing.\nsokolova neutral: Then we sail for Port Anselm. Bartholomew, wake the engineer. Child, sleep.\nvale neutral: I can't sleep. Can I ask you something, Quill? Thirty-one years. Why did you go?\nquill neutral: I didn't, Doctor. She did. Theatre Three still has her apron on the hook.\n`;\nS.c10_before = `\n@card Chapter 4|Coda\n@stage ward\npercy neutral: Vale. You're back. Everyone's been... it's fine. Everything's fine. I wrote it all up.\nvale neutral: Percy, you look awful. When did you last sleep? Your eyes are doing something.\npercy smile: Do you know, I've never told you this. You've got better hands than I have. It's fine.\npercy smile: It doesn't bother me. Nothing bothers me. Isn't that... oh. It's finally quiet.\n@cine percycollapse\nvale shock: Percy! PERCY! Somebody! He's still smiling, why is he still smiling!\n@stage radiology\nodalys sad: Okay. So. He charted himself. In his own hand. It says migraine. He's been on Lull since March.\nodalys neutral: Look at the film. Point at it. I'm not going to say what I think it is. It's looking at me.\nsokolova neutral: Two surgeons, child. One table. Try not to be sentimental. I shall take the first cut.\n`;\nS.c10_after = `\n@stage ward\nvale sad: Percy? Percy, it's Immy. Can you hear me? Do you know where you are?\npercy angry: Your stitches... are crooked.\nvale smile: They are. They're so crooked. I did them crooked. I'm so sorry. I'm crying, this is crying.\npercy sad: It hurts, Vale. Everything hurts. I've been so very frightened. For such a long time.\npercy neutral: I should've said. It was never a hernia. Do you want that in writing?\nquill neutral: In pen, Doctor.\nsokolova neutral: Good. Now the bad news, children. Look at the ER board.\nodalys shock: Okay so everybody freak out. Every Anodyne patient in the city's failing. All of them. At once.\n`;\nS.c11_before = `\n@stage ward\nhale sad: Please. Someone. Please. She's stopped answering. Someone please take her.\nvale shock: Dr. Hale. You came here? You carried her? In the rain? All the way from the east wing?\nhale sad: I only wanted her not to hurt. Doctor, you have to understand. I only wanted her not to hurt.\nclementine smile: Hello. It's all right, Father. Everyone's singing. Can you hear it? It's so beautiful now.\nsokolova neutral: It's wrapped around her heart, Lucian. It's conducting the whole city from in there.\nhale sad: Pain is the last disease, Doctor. I only ask you to stop defending it. Save her. Please.\n@cine finaltable\noyelaran neutral: I'm in the gallery. I've got a wheelchair and an opinion. Do not make me use either.\npercy neutral: I've got the monitors. Your pressure readings'll be right. For once. In pen.\nodalys smile: I've got the film and six donuts. Okay so don't freak out.\nquill neutral: Scalpel, Doctors. Whichever of you. I've passed instruments to four of you now.\nvale neutral: Don't let it go quiet, Quill.\nquill smile: No, Doctor. Not tonight.\n`;\nS.c11_after = `\n@stage theatre\nclementine shock: AAH! AAAH! Oh, it, oh, what, oh, Father, it, I...\nclementine smile: I can feel my hands. I can feel my hands! They hurt! Is this what it... they're so warm!\nhale sad: Clementine. Oh, my girl. Oh, there you are. I'm so sorry. There you are.\n@cine erchaos\n@stage ward\nagnes angry: Doctor! I have a headache! My whole arm aches! I demand to be seen!\nvale smile: Wonderful!\nslide angry: My spit valve hurts! I don't know how! I want my money back!\nvale smile: That's wonderful! Next!\nned angry: There was a GAFF in me! Who authorized that! I felt NOTHING and I demand to feel it now!\noyelaran smile: Listen to them. The loudest, angriest, happiest ward in the North Atlantic.\nhale neutral: I'll be in the east wing, Doctors, when the police arrive. I'll leave the doors unlocked.\nhale neutral: Doctor? You were right to defend it. I still wish that you hadn't had to.\nsokolova smile: Well, Bartholomew. Apron's still on the hook, I hear.\nquill neutral: Thirty-one years, Yevgenia. I've had it washed.\nvale neutral: Quill. Look. My hands. I think they've...\nquill neutral: They've stopped shaking, Doctor.\nvale neutral: ...No, they haven't.\nquill smile: Good.\n`;\n})();\nEOF\ncat > src/data/story/flow.js <<'EOF'\n// Compiles the script format into E.Story nodes, and lays out the story's order. Adding a case: a script, a line here.\n(() => {\nconst G = (window.GAME ||= {});\nG.FLOW = []; for (let i = 1; i <= 11; i++) { G.FLOW.push({ k: 'story', id: 'c' + i + '_before' }); G.FLOW.push({ k: 'case', id: 'c' + i }); G.FLOW.push({ k: 'story', id: 'c' + i + '_after' }); if (i < 11) G.FLOW.push({ k: 'ward', map: i >= 6 && i <= 8 ? 'ship' : 'ward3', after: i }); } G.FLOW.push({ k: 'credits' });\nG.compileStory = () => { const nodes = {}; for (const id in G.SCRIPT) { const out = []; let extra = 0; const lines = G.SCRIPT[id].split('\\n').map(l => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);\n    lines.forEach((l) => { if (l[0] === '@') { const sp = l.indexOf(' '), k = l.slice(1, sp), v = l.slice(sp + 1); if (k === 'card') out.push({ event: 'card', arg: v, wait: true }); else if (k === 'stage') out.push({ event: 'stage', arg: v }); else if (k === 'cine') out.push({ event: 'cine', arg: v, wait: true }); return; }\n      if (l[0] === '?') { const opts = l.slice(1).split('|').map(s => s.trim().split('=>').map(q => q.trim())), back = id + '#' + (extra++); const prev = out[out.length - 1]; const who = 'vale'; const choices = opts.map(([text, reply], i) => { const n = back + 'r' + i; nodes[n] = [{ event: 'look', who: 'quill', expr: 'neutral' }, { who: 'quill', say: reply }, { goto: back }]; return { text, goto: n }; }); out.push({ event: 'look', who: 'vale', expr: 'neutral' }); out.push({ who: 'vale', say: '...', choices }); nodes[back] = []; out.push({ __cont: back }); return; }\n      const m = l.match(/^(\\w+) (\\w+): (.*)$/); if (!m) throw new Error('bad script line: ' + l); out.push({ event: 'look', who: m[1], expr: m[2] }); out.push({ who: m[1], say: m[3] }); });\n    // a choice's replies come back to the rest of the scene\n    let cur = out, res = []; const finish = (arr, name) => { nodes[name] = arr; };\n    const flat = []; let target = id; nodes[target] = flat; for (const st of out) { if (st.__cont) { const nx = nodes[st.__cont]; target = st.__cont; nodes[target] = nx; cur = nx; continue; } (target === id ? flat : nodes[target]).push(st); } }\n  return nodes; };\nG.WARD_LINES = {\n  quill: ['Knit one. Purl one. Rest, Doctor. The next one is always worse.', 'I am making a scarf. It is for no one. It has been for no one since 1994.', 'Morale, Doctor. I have heard of it.'],\n  odalys: ['Okay so I have a theory, and it has a corkboard. Donut?', 'The vending machine hums in B-flat. I checked it. It is just a vending machine. I think.', 'I found your scan. You have nice bones, Immy. I mean that professionally.'],\n  percy: ['The machine took my coin. It would not dare take a second one.', 'Shouldn\\'t you be labeling something, Vale? I have a resection to think about.', 'I have not had a tremor in months. It is all in the wrists.', 'It is odd. It has been so quiet in here lately.'],\n  chief: ['The door says Do Not Knock. It has said so for eleven years.', 'A dry cough, from behind the door: \"Go and do something useful, Dr. Vale.\"', 'The cane is in the umbrella stand. That has never happened before.'],\n  agnes: ['The grandchildren brought begonias. They are enormous. I told them to put them by the glass.', 'The heron is coming along. It has one leg so far. Herons do, mostly.', 'Are you eating, dear? You look like you are eating a very small amount.', 'That arm aches when the rain comes in. I rather like knowing the weather.'],\n  slide: ['I practice with the mute in. Your nurse tells me it is an improvement.', 'I have had the cork mounted. It is on the bell of the horn. Very classy.', 'Long notes hold now. Forty seconds. Don\\'t tell my cardiologist.'],\n  tam: ['Tell you what, wee doctor. That crane is getting a stern talking to.', 'Morag brought the tea. It was late, but so was I.'],\n  ned: ['I can feel the tide in this side now. It is a nuisance. Very reliable.', 'No more whistling. It hurts when I whistle. I whistle anyway.'],\n  ilse: ['It hurts to hold the bow. I played for an hour. It was the worst hour. Listen to this.', 'Mother cried. I think I cried. I can tell now.'],\n  yusuf: ['I have asked for a third sandwich. The doctora says it is a medical necessity.', 'The ship rolls and I feel it in the leg. Now I always know the weather.'],\n  perpetua: ['I have not forgiven her. I would like you to tell her so.', 'Sit a moment, child. You look as though you have been holding something. Hand it here.'],\n  crane: ['I keep putting my hand on my chest. There is only me in there now.', 'I would like to sign something. A confession, a petition. Anything.'],\n};\nG.WARD_PEOPLE = { 1: ['agnes'], 2: ['agnes', 'slide'], 3: ['agnes', 'slide', 'tam'], 4: ['slide', 'tam', 'ned'], 5: ['tam', 'ned', 'ilse'], 6: ['ned', 'ilse'], 7: ['yusuf'], 8: ['yusuf', 'perpetua'], 9: ['agnes', 'ilse', 'crane'], 10: ['agnes', 'slide', 'tam', 'ned', 'ilse', 'yusuf', 'crane'] };\n})();\nEOF\ncat > src/data/barks.js <<'EOF'\n// Quill's lines by situation. Barks never pause play, and a shared pool keeps them from repeating fast.\n(() => {\nconst G = (window.GAME ||= {});\nG.BARKS = {\n  slip: ['That was the liver, Doctor. We keep that one.', 'A bold line. Not the one we drew.', 'I have seen worse. He was a plumber.', 'Noted, Doctor. I shall not write it down.', 'The other way would have been traditional.', 'Mm.', 'Breathe, Doctor. One of you should.', 'She will not feel that. You might.'],\n  tear: ['Slower. It is not going anywhere.', 'It tore. They do that when hurried.', 'That came out in two parts, Doctor.', 'Along the axis. The way it went in.'],\n  low: ['Pulse forty. I mention it only in passing.', 'Getting quiet, Doctor. Syringe.', 'I would dose the IV now, if I were you.', 'Thirty and falling. No rush. Some rush.', 'Stimulant, Doctor. He is using his up.'],\n  textbook: ['...Hm.', 'Acceptable.', 'I have seen that done worse. Rarely.', 'I shall tell no one. They would only ask you to do it again.', 'Nice.', 'That one goes in the book, Doctor.'],\n  idle: ['Any time, Doctor.', 'The next tool, if it pleases you.', 'I could recite something, while we wait.', 'He is not getting any younger.'],\n  phase: ['Next.', 'Good. Keep going.', 'Moving on.', 'That is one thing fixed. There are others.'],\n  flow: ['You are in it now, Doctor. Do not look down.', 'Steady hands tonight.', 'Keep that going.'],\n  heat: ['It runs hot, Doctor. Let it rest.', 'That is the cautery telling you something.'],\n  revived: ['Welcome back, sir. Doctor, you may breathe now.', 'He is with us. Do not make a habit of it.'],\n  gift: ['We don\\'t discuss it in the lift.', 'There it is.'],\n  look: ['No. Again.', 'At my age, everything happens twice.', 'I\\'ve seen this one.'],\n  bite: ['Something bit him. He has not noticed.', 'They are landing, Doctor. Watch the rings.'],\n  readout: ['{v}, Doctor.', 'Pulse {v}.', '{v}. Holding.', 'I have {v}, Doctor.'],\n  wrongwire: ['That was not the wire, Doctor. It is still a ship. For now.'],\n  loose: ['Loose. Take it out. Do it again.', 'That will not hold, Doctor.'],\n  close: ['Dressing.'],\n};\nG.LOST_LINES = { vitals: ['Time of death, oh-four-twelve. Shall we go again, Doctor?', 'He is gone, Doctor. Take a breath. Take it again.'], time: ['Time of death, oh-four-twelve.', 'We ran out of night, Doctor.'], bomb: ['The ship went, Doctor. I had just washed that apron. Again?'] };\nG.RANK_LINES = { S: ['I have nothing to add. Mark the date.', 'I passed instruments to three who could do that. Four.'], A: ['Very nearly dull, Doctor. That is high praise.', 'I would let you operate on my cat.'], B: ['It held. Most things do, if you stitch them.', 'A solid night. Go and shake somewhere private.'], C: ['The patient lived. We shall lead with that.', 'I have seen worse. He was a plumber.'], D: ['Alive is a grade, Doctor. It is the lowest one.', 'I shall file this under \"Eventually\".'] };\n})();\nEOF\n","description":"Write full story scripts, flow compiler and barks"}}}
