{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkXTTyxECcQVgHgpnR3V","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Second Scalpel is complete: everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks for is in the `work` folder.** The final game file (hash `b4f9a8058473`) passes the full test command with 1803 checks and 0 failed.\n\n## Evidence for the last open points\n- **First clear, now measured:** `tools/firstclear.mjs` plays the whole game through its real scenes on game time at a first-timer's pace, and reaches the end with no errors. It takes **59.3 minutes** without failed tries, against the plan's \"about an hour\". With that run's 12 losses it takes 116.5 minutes, which fits the plan's expectation of repeated tries on the hard cases.\n- **Time per scene in that run, including failed tries:**\n\n  | Scene | Minutes |\n  |---|---|\n  | Operations | 92.5 |\n  | Story | 14 |\n  | Ward | 5.6 |\n  | Chart | 1.4 |\n  | Results | 1.2 |\n\n- **Tries in that run:** Cases 1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 9 and 10 clear first try, in 3.0–3.6 minutes. Case 3 and Tremolo take 3 tries, Case 8 takes 6 and the Cantor takes 4.\n- **Plan audit:** `notes/plan-audit.md` covers sections 0–22, and no item relies on code reading any more. Grand Rounds awards, standings and a player leaving are tested against the Hub's finish call, and attract is tested from Case Files and the Lobby. What remains unscripted is what TASK.md says to judge by screenshot, such as how art and motion look.\n\n## What you should know\n- **Nobody has played it.** Hand feel and difficulty rest on bots and stand-ins, because this computer has no controller, listener, real iPhone or TV. TASK.md says those are \"never required\". A session on a real DualSense is the most useful next step, starting with Case 1.\n- **Numbers changed from the plan:** clocks, object counts, some damage values and the baton rest. All are listed in `notes/plan-audit.md`.\n- **Lobby is at 86 colors:** the tool's look check passes (it warns below 40), but that's under its \"100+\" remark about PS1-era scenes.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 06:13:00\n- **Source:** Trauma Center: Second Opinion (Atlus, Wii, 2006)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Surgical action game with story scenes and a small walkable ward. First-person top-down on the operative field; front-on portrait stages for story; three-quarter view for the ward and cinematics; side view for the title.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.89.1 (handoff package, full engine, one patch: the touch notice), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, Web Audio, Node and Playwright 1.63 for tests\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's pixel look at PS1 level: per-pixel wet tissue through hand-picked ramps with ordered dithering, flat-shaded bust portraits and painted stages, all by code; new hospital shapes as data; asset store for three generic furniture props (Kenney, CC0)\n- **Animation:** The engine's Humanoid rigs and continuous-value animation, with new poses (scrub-in, gurney push, leaning over the table, passing an instrument, knitting) and a surgical cap; procedural organs, parasites and instruments\n- **Audio:** All in code on Web Audio: synthesized effects, a parasite choir, Animalese-style gibberish voices per character, songs in the engine's notation with music at 10% by default\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, shared world, 1 to 4 players; couch co-op and AI colleagues on its own\n- **Testing:** Headless scripts that play the game and check state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, behind one command printing only failures and a count; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit\n- **Research:** None beyond the plan's own (Wikipedia and a Retro XP essay, read for the plan)\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** 43 issues: 32 from bugs the scripted tests and bot runs caught and layout and art problems in screenshots, and 11 more from line-by-line audits of the plan, which are now tested where a script can.\n- **How many were critical or game breaking?** 6 would have been game breaking, such as a syntax error of mine that stopped the page loading, a Tremolo softlock when half doses never reached the crack, a retractor hold that crashed Grand Rounds, and a scene field that crashed on re-entry.\n- **Most helpful test:** The bot playing each case to its end, and the scripted full playthrough built on it: it found every softlock. A slower first-timer bot was next best: it showed the cases were too short and the swarm too deadly.\n- **Least helpful test:** The cross-browser run: three browsers playing with no errors never caught anything, though it was worth having as a guard.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** Exact fixed steps with headless advance: the whole game plays in Node in seconds, so the sim could be tested long before anything was drawn. The standard pause, settings, rebinding and prompts also saved a great deal of work.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, a few times. A scene field named `view` is read as an engine option, which crashed on re-entry. `Humanoid.hat` takes one object, not the canvas signature I guessed. Both were in the index; I hadn't looked.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The sim itself: one JSON-safe state with the eight tool gestures, rewind, checkpoints and network mirroring all resting on it. The tissue painter from the plan carries the look, but the operation is all game code.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Knowing what the engine already has: `API.md` is dense, and the answer is often one line in a 200 KB index. Keeping `E.Dialog`'s and `E.Story`'s flow in step with my own stage and portraits also took care.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** One patch: the touch notice in `Game.prototype._needSync` no longer covers the screen, so the attract reel shows above it, and its Settings button keeps pointer events. TASK.md wants the attract running on touch-only devices.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** A scene option that clashes with a scene's own field name should warn at definition, not fail on the second entry.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** The plan is unusually concrete: verified engine facts, reference code that ran on this computer, and a clear rule for what wins when documents disagree. Almost nothing had to be rediscovered.\n- **What was confusing?** TASK.md says one exit interview shape and the plan repeats it, which was clear. The less clear part was the plan's folder layout against its own size: a few files were sensibly merged, and nothing says whether that is acceptable.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Scope: eleven cases, six strains, both gifts, a ward, a Hub mode and an attract reel for one builder. Getting the feel right is the hardest part to check, because nobody can actually hold a controller here; I could only test the numbers and read screenshots.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** Ask for less content and more hours of feel: a human or a recorded hand on the stick is what this game most lacks. Say explicitly that hand feel can only be checked by bot here, so it is reported as untested by a person.\n\n## Files\nEverything is in the `work` folder. To run the tests, use `node tests/run.mjs` from the dev folder; adding `--fast` skips the browser and Hub parts. I haven't committed anything to git, since you didn't ask me to.\n- `SecondScalpel-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-061300.html`\n- `exit-interview.json`\n- `SecondScalpel-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261105-061300/` (dev files, including `CONTENT.md`, `notes/plan-audit.md`, `notes/checklist.md` and `tools/firstclear.mjs`)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":250,"cache_read_input_tokens":781689,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":250},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c4983f3c-8ab3-4cb5-bbfd-23a240d1dce6","uuid":"f0b83df2-ebee-41d4-9613-795619c25626","timestamp":"2026-11-05T21:55:12.171Z","request_id":"req_011CgkXTTatFA3jjjeuuw1KY"}
